uloop: install the SIGCHLD wake handler with SA_RESTART

uloop installs its wake-up signal handlers with sa_flags = 0.  SIGCHLD is not
a cancellation request: it is the bookkeeping signal the uloop_process
watchers use to learn that a child exited.  Without SA_RESTART a child that
exits while a daemon handler is blocked in read(2) makes that read fail with
EINTR.

rpcd runs the LuCI RPC handlers (ucode, via rpcd-mod-ucode) in that same main
loop, and luci.getProcessList() blocks in getline() on the stdout pipe of
`/bin/busybox top -bn1`, which is a child of rpcd itself.  Busybox top spends
its first ~100 ms sampling without printing (the batch loop drops the first
iteration to get a CPU baseline), so that first read is blocked exactly when
top flushes its output and exits; when the SIGCHLD wins the wake-up race,
getline() returns EINTR, ucode's stdio keeps the sticky error flag, the read
returns null and luci-base's process_list() returns an empty list.  LuCI then
renders "no information available" on Status -> Processes (seen on the RD03v2
DEV-pkgs-1028 image; luci-lib-jsonc is installed in it and unrelated).

SIGINT and SIGTERM keep sa_flags = 0: those do ask the process to stop, so
letting them interrupt a blocking call is the intent.

--- a/uloop.c
+++ b/uloop.c
@@ -546,7 +546,7 @@
 		if (s.sa_handler == SIG_DFL) { /* Do not override existing custom signal handlers */
 			memcpy(old, &s, sizeof(struct sigaction));
 			s.sa_handler = handler;
-			s.sa_flags = 0;
+			s.sa_flags = (signum == SIGCHLD) ? SA_RESTART : 0;
 			act = &s;
 		}
 	}
@@ -616,7 +616,7 @@
 
 	if (s->orig.sa_handler != uloop_signal_wake) {
 		sa.sa_handler = uloop_signal_wake;
-		sa.sa_flags = 0;
+		sa.sa_flags = (s->signo == SIGCHLD) ? SA_RESTART : 0;
 		sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
 		sigaction(s->signo, &sa, NULL);
 	}
